      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e\u003e and set it only later in vmx_vcpu_create.\n\n\u003e You mean in attach_pkvm_vcpu_to_vm()?\n\nSorry for not being clear. I was mentioning of setting pid_table in vmx_vcpu_create:\n\n```\n»       if (vmx_can_use_ipiv(vcpu))\n#ifndef __PKVM_HYP__\n»       »       WRITE_ONCE(to_kvm_vmx(vcpu-\u003ekvm)-\u003epid_table[vcpu-\u003evcpu_id],\n»       »       »          __pa(\u0026vmx-\u003evt.pi_desc) | PID_TABLE_ENTRY_VALID);\n#else\n»       »       WRITE_ONCE(to_kvm_vmx(vcpu-\u003ekvm)-\u003epid_table[vcpu-\u003evcpu_id],\n»       »       »          __pa(\u0026shared_vmx-\u003evt.pi_desc) | PID_TABLE_ENTRY_VALID);\n#endif\n```\n\nAttach issue you mentioned is more important one. \n\n\u003e Although, after more thinking, maybe a simpler and cleaner way is to move this validation of duplicated vcpu_id into attach_pkvm_vcpu_to_vm(), i.e. after vCPU creation. That would make pKVM\u0027s flow even closer to the upstream flow in kvm_vm_ioctl_create_vcpu(). Although that would make it affected by the upstream issue we found yesterday (i.e. corrupting pid_table entry of another vCPU with the same vcpu_id). But I have an idea how to fix it: move pid_table entry setup from .vcpu_create() to a new .vcpu_postcreate() callback invoked at the very end of successful vCPU creation (in particular, in our pKVM case, after successful attach_pkvm_vcpu_to_vm()).\n\nThis is a good idea. We should probably be careful in the cleanup path(of a duplicate vcpu_id failure) to not unconidtionally zero out the pid_table in vmx_vcpu_free:\n```\n#ifdef __PKVM_HYP__\n        if (vmx_can_use_ipiv(vcpu))\n                WRITE_ONCE(to_kvm_vmx(vcpu-\u003ekvm)-\u003epid_table[vcpu-\u003evcpu_id], 0);\n#endif\n```\n\nAlso not sure if the delayed setting of pid_table after attach would cause any race. Is the vcpu usable after an attach that could cause the vcpu to run(by a malicious host) before finishing postcreate?",
      "parentUuid": "33dc242f_26431a00",
      "revId": "3bf049dd7d9d09e0677a18256c8f1d551204ff35",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
